WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.728
Idea. At a theme park you must be taller than one metre, and shorter than two metres. Only people who pass every rule ride. On a graph, an inequality is a rule about x and y. The region is every point that passes all the rules.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:26.528
One inequality splits the grid in two. Its line is the boundary. Points on one side make the inequality true. Points on the other side make it false.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:38.624
To find the true side, use a test point. Pick a point that is not on the line, such as the origin. Put it in the inequality. If it is true, shade the side with that point.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:47.747
The question says: show, by shading, the region R. You shade the one region where every inequality is true, and write R in it.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:01:01.237
Method. Step one: take each inequality in turn and pick a test point not on its line. The origin is best. Step two: say whether the test point is true, and mark the wanted side.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:10.727
Step three: find the part of the grid on the wanted side of every line. It is usually a triangle. Step four: shade it and label it R.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:18.160
The trap is testing a point on the line. It always fits a less than or equal sign, so it tells you nothing.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:30.499
Worked example. June twenty twenty three, part b. Shade the region R defined by these three inequalities. The lines are drawn already, from part a. One mark.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:45.660
Test the origin in each. Nought is less than or equal to five: true. Nought is greater than or equal to minus eight: true. Nought is less than or equal to thirty: true. So R is on the origin side.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:54.953
On the grid, the origin is below line one, above line two, and below line three. R is the region on that side of all three.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:09.429
The lines cross at three corners: minus four, minus three; five over thirteen, seventy five over thirteen; and one hundred and sixty over seventeen, six over seventeen. You do not need them to shade.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:20.164
A tempting wrong route is shading below all three lines. But the origin is above line two, and the test said true. So the origin must be inside R.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:28.506
So shade the triangle and write R in it. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:41.083
Common mistake. A common mistake is to test a point on the line. Try nought, five. Five is less than or equal to five, which is true on both sides, so it proves nothing.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:58.167
Your turn. Now you try, from January twenty twenty. Part a drew five x plus two y equals ten, and y equals x. Shade and label the region R for these four inequalities. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:19.823
Answer. First draw the missing lines, y equals minus two and x equals one. The origin lies on y equals x, so test the point two, nought for that line. Nought is less than or equal to two: true. For the other three, the origin gives true, true, and false for x greater than or equal to one.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:34.648
So R is below y equals x, below the line five x plus two y equals ten, above y equals minus two, and to the right of x equals one. It has four sides. Shade it and write R inside it.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:50.323
The common wrong answer tests the origin in y less than or equal to x. The origin lies on that line, so the test proves nothing, and the region is often shaded above the line. Forgetting to write R also loses a mark.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:57.268
Remember: test a point off the line for each inequality, find the overlap, shade it and label R.
